        "en": "Holding companies oversee group management by holding equity stakes in subsidiaries, meaning their performance is structurally tied to the net asset value (NAV) and dividend flows of their subsidiaries. Korea has more than 100 listed holding companies, yet their scale varies enormously, ranging from pure or intermediate holding companies of major conglomerates such as SK, LG, GS, Hanjin KAL, and Doosan to entities with stronger operating-company characteristics like POSCO Holdings and Ecopro. Korean holding companies have traditionally been characterized by a chronic 'NAV discount,' where share prices trade well below the sum of subsidiary values. Recently, changes in governance-related legislation have emerged as a key variable altering this discount structure. While the sector includes a large number of listed names, market capitalization is highly concentrated, with a handful of top constituents driving overall sector momentum."
