        "en": "Domestic petrochemical companies are pursuing capacity cuts and integration to resolve structural oversupply, which is seen as a key point to watch for future industry recovery. In 2026, whether capacity reduction implementation at the country's three major industrial complexes translates into earnings improvement is expected to be the key issue. In particular, the integration of Yeosu NCC facilities with GS Caltex is seen as a key to resolving the structural problem, with the two companies having submitted a restructuring plan to the government to halt aging facility operations; while closure of Yeosu Plant No.1 had been considered likely, recent reports suggest greater weight is being given to integrating the already-shut Plant No.2 instead. At the same time, tightening circular economy and de-plastic regulations are emerging as a new variable, with Korea mandating from January 2026 that large manufacturers using more than 5,000 tons annually of clear PET bottles use at least 10% recycled content, with the scope and mandate rate set to expand through 2030. Against this backdrop, companies such as Lotte Fine Chemical, which have business structures allowing for greater product differentiation than commodity petrochemicals, are seen as capable of relatively higher resilience during industry downturns. Ultimately, the pace of restructuring in commodity segments and the success of portfolio shifts toward specialty and materials businesses are likely to be the key factors differentiating individual company performance within the sector."
